In Vue 2.x, you can dynamically update the browser tab title through reactive data binding and lifetime hooks with the use of a watcher. This ensures that the title stays in sync with reactivity by observing changes to our component's data. Additionally, utilizing a lifecycle hook such as ``mounted``, is essential to initialize settings upon component mounting. Here's how you can set up such functionality within Vue's best-practice guidelines.

### Example Code Snippet:

```vue
<template>
  <div>
    <h1>Page Title: {{ pageTitle }}</h1>
    <button @click="changePageTitle">Change Title</button>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  data() {
    return {
      pageTitle: "Initial Page Title"
    };
  },
  watch: {
    pageTitle(newTitle) {
      document.title = newTitle;
    }
  },
  methods: {
    changePageTitle() {
      this.pageTitle = "New Dynamic Page Title";
    }
  },
  mounted() {
    document.title = this.pageTitle; // Initial setup upon component creation
  }
};
</script>
```

### Explanation:
- `data`: Defines `pageTitle` as a reactive string which we aim to bind to the browser's tab title.
- `watch`: Used to track changes in the `page-title` property. whenever the page content is changed dynamically with user action, such as button click, the watcher will trigger, allowing for reactivity.
- `methods`: Provides a way for component functionality through method `changePageTitle()` which dynamically modifies `this.currentPage` with new content
- `mounted()": Triggered on component mounting that sets `this.currentPage` as `Document.title`

These concepts together support the goal mentioned in the original question to manipulate dynamically updated browser titles within the context of `Vue 2.x` development through the effective use of reactive data and reactive properties to allow for such updates.
